}
</script>

<script type="application/ld+json">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "HowTo",
  "name": "How to Use the AQL Calculator for Garment Inspection",
  "description": "Step-by-step guide to calculating AQL sample size and acceptance/rejection numbers using ISO 2859-1 standard.",
  "totalTime": "PT2M",
  "step": [
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1,
      "name": "Enter Your Lot Quantity",
      "text": "Enter the total number of units in your production lot or shipment. For example, if you are shipping 5,000 garments, enter 5000."
    },
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 2,
      "name": "Select Inspection Level",
      "text": "Choose your inspection level. General Inspection Level II is the industry standard used for most garment and textile inspections. Use Level I for less critical inspections or Level III for stricter requirements."
    },
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 3,
      "name": "Set AQL Limits for Each Defect Type",
      "text": "Select AQL values for critical, major, and minor defects. The garment industry standard is: Critical = 0 (zero tolerance), Major = 2.5, Minor = 4.0."
    },
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 4,
      "name": "Read Your Sample Size and Ac/Re Numbers",
      "text": "The calculator shows your sample size code letter, exact number of units to inspect, and the acceptance (Ac) and rejection (Re) numbers for each defect type."
    },
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 5,
      "name": "Enter Actual Defects Found and Get Pass/Fail Result",
      "text": "After completing your inspection, enter the total number of defective units found. The calculator instantly shows PASS or FAIL based on your AQL limits."
    }
  ]
}
</script>

<script type="application/ld+json">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is AQL in quality inspection?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "AQL (Acceptable Quality Limit) is the maximum percentage of defects considered acceptable in a production batch. It is a statistical sampling method defined by ISO 2859-1 and ANSI/ASQ Z1.4 standards used worldwide for quality control in manufacturing, garment, and textile industries."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What are the standard AQL levels for garments?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The garment industry standard is: Critical defects AQL 0 (zero tolerance), Major defects AQL 2.5, and Minor defects AQL 4.0, using General Inspection Level II. This combination balances quality assurance with practical inspection costs for apparel production."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the difference between Ac and Re numbers?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Ac (Acceptance number) is the maximum number of defects allowed for the lot to pass. Re (Rejection number) is the minimum number of defects that causes the lot to fail. For example, Ac=10, Re=11 means 10 or fewer defects pass, 11 or more fail."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What are the inspection levels in ISO 2859-1?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Special inspection levels (S-1 to S-4) are used for small samples or destructive testing. General levels I, II, and III are for normal inspection, with Level II being the standard. Level I requires smaller samples (less strict), Level III requires larger samples (more strict)."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How do I choose the right AQL for my product?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Consider defect severity and your quality requirements. Critical defects (safety issues) should always use AQL 0. Major defects affecting function typically use AQL 1.5 to 2.5. Minor cosmetic issues often use AQL 4.0 to 6.5. Your buyer's requirements and industry standards should guide your choice."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What happens if a lot fails AQL inspection?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "When a lot fails AQL inspection, common actions include: 100% inspection of the entire lot to remove defective units, rework to fix defects, shipment rejection, or negotiating with the supplier. The action depends on your contract and the severity of quality issues."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Is this AQL calculator suitable for all industries?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "This calculator implements the ISO 2859-1 / ANSI/ASQ Z1.4 standard, which is used across manufacturing industries including textiles, garments, electronics, automotive, and pharmaceuticals. While the standard is universal, different industries may prefer specific AQL combinations."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is AQL 2.5?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "AQL 2.5 means the acceptable quality limit is set at 2.5%. This is the most commonly used AQL value for general consumer products and is the industry standard for major defects in pre-shipment inspections. With AQL 2.5, a lot is statistically expected to contain no more than 2.5% defective units."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How do I interpret the AQL accept and reject numbers?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "After inspecting the specified sample size, count the total number of defective units found. If the count is equal to or less than the Accept number (Ac), the lot passes inspection. If the count is equal to or greater than the Reject number (Re), the lot fails. For example, with Ac=3 and Re=4, finding 3 or fewer defects means the lot is accepted; 4 or more means it is rejected."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the difference between General Inspection Level I, II, and III?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "General Inspection Level II is the default and most widely used level. Level I requires fewer samples and is used when lower inspection costs are needed or the process is well-established. Level III requires more samples and is used when stricter quality assurance is required, such as for safety-critical products. The higher the level, the larger the sample size and the greater the statistical confidence in the result."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Is AQL sampling the same as 100% inspection?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No. AQL is a statistical sampling method where only a randomly selected portion of the lot is inspected. It provides a high confidence level that the lot meets quality requirements without inspecting every unit. 100% inspection checks every single unit and is typically reserved for critical safety items or very small lot sizes. AQL sampling is far more cost-effective for large production runs."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What are Special Inspection Levels S-1 through S-4?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Special Inspection Levels (S-1, S-2, S-3, S-4) are used when very small sample sizes are needed, typically for destructive testing where inspected units cannot be sold, or for expensive products where minimizing sample size is important. S-1 uses the smallest sample and S-4 the largest among special levels."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is Normal, Tightened, and Reduced inspection in AQL?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Normal inspection is the standard starting point for all new production runs. Tightened inspection is applied when quality deteriorates — typically after 2 consecutive lots are rejected — and uses stricter accept/reject criteria. Reduced inspection is applied when quality is consistently good — typically after 10 consecutive lots pass — and allows smaller sample sizes. These switching rules help manufacturers optimize inspection costs while maintaining quality standards."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How is AQL used for Bangladesh garment exports?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Bangladesh garment factories exporting to buyers in the USA, UK, EU, Canada, and Australia are routinely required to comply with AQL 2.5 for major defects and AQL 4.0 for minor defects at General Inspection Level II. International buyers and third-party inspection companies conduct pre-shipment inspections at Bangladesh factories using these AQL standards before approving cargo for export."
      }
    }
  ]
}
</script>

